  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nUpper trough will be moving out today, with only a few lingering \nshowers possible in the morning hours in far northeast Tennessee and \nnear 81 in Virginia. Otherwise clouds will be clearing from west to \neast through the day. Temperatures likewise will begin the rebound \nto above normal. This trend continues through Thursday as the jet \nstream moves off to the north and a summer strength upper ridge sets \nup and dominates over the southern US. The ridge's strength also \nmeans dry weather through Thursday.\n\nWith dewpoints also remaining elevated and our recent rainfall, we \nshould see patchy fog for the next couple mornings. Some patchy fog \npossible this morning, but certainly expect to see more widespread \nfog Tuesday morning.\n\nHeading into late week we continue to see signs of a significant \ntemperature change for the weekend. Friday a weak front will pass \nby, and the accompanying rainfall is very little. However, the \nairmass change behind the front is significant, knocking the wider \nregion from its above normal temperature stance back into a true \ntaste of fall for mid October. Saturday's highs are in the low 60s, \nnow below average, and Sunday morning has 50% (or higher) chances \nfrom Morgan County to Hamblen and north to be in the temperature \nrange for frost. For the Tri-Cities, this is right on time to \nexperience the first bout of temperatures this cold for the fall. \nSeasonal plants beware, your time outdoors is quickly coming to an \nend for this year.",
